  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 208 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nForecast gets interesting as we head into the weekend and into early \nnext week. A 500 mb low will form in the vicinity of Cuba and strong \nsurface high pressure will be in place over the northeast US. Deep \nmoisture will be in place over SE FL for multiple days with PWAT \nvalues increasing to 1.8-2.0 inches. Rounds of heavy rainfall appear \nlikely especially on Saturday and Sunday, but may continue into \nMonday as well. Mean rainfall totals from Friday through Monday show \n3-6 inches for the east coast metro, with worst case scenario values \nover 10 inches possible. Given the peak of the current king tide \ncycle is also this weekend, the potential exists for some serious \nflooding concerns across the metro and other poor drainage \nlocations along the coast. Should the forecast remain the same \nover the coming days, Flood headlines would be very likely. \n\nIn addition to the heavy rain threat, the tightening of the pressure \ngradient this weekend across FL will result in breezy NE winds, \nwith gusts of 30-40 mph likely, especially along the coast. \n\nConditions will improve for Tuesday into mid week as the upper low \nopens up and lifts up to the northeast and drier air moves back into \nthe area. \n\nTemperatures during the extended period will see high temps in the \nlow to mid 80s each day and lows in the 60s/70s.",
